  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 045: Network interfaces move data out of servers, while switches choose shared paths that can become bottlenecks.

Lesson 046: Copper works well over short connections, while optics carries signals reliably over longer distances.

Lesson 047: Synchronized workers depend on one another, so one slow or failed participant can waste time across the cluster.

Lesson 048: More accelerators help only when their useful output justifies the extra communication, waiting and cost.

Lesson 049: Dense AI racks need enough electricity and cooling at every step, or expensive chips cannot run as intended.

Two power feeds. One outage.

An AI rack has two electrical feeds, labelled A and B. One morning, an upstream breaker opens and both feeds go dark. Nothing inside the servers has failed. Why did the backup disappear with the main supply?

In this illustrative incident, the two feeds share that breaker. There are two paths near the rack, but a common point of failure farther upstream. The labels describe the final connections; they do not prove independence.

Lesson 049 followed electricity into the server. Now we work backward through the building to understand what makes that electricity available, controllable and resilient. The goal is to read a power-capacity claim and know what still needs checking.

Start upstream of the rack

Before electricity reaches a server, the facility must receive it, change voltage, distribute it and protect the equipment along the way. A transformer changes AC voltage. Switchgear contains switching and protective equipment. An uninterruptible power supply, or UPS, supports critical loads through interruptions.

These are different jobs. Owning one component does not substitute for the others. Here is one simplified arrangement; real facilities vary in equipment order, voltage levels and the location of backup connections.

Utility supply — brings electricity to the site

↓

Switchgear and transformers — protect circuits and change voltage

↓

UPS and downstream distribution — bridge interruptions and carry power onward

↓

Rack feeds and server supplies — deliver usable power to compute

A generator can connect through transfer or paralleling equipment to support an appropriate upstream point. It is an alternative source, not a component that electricity must always pass through. The exact connections determine which failures the system can survive.

The transformer solves a transport problem

Large amounts of power are easier to move at higher voltage because the same power requires less current, other factors held constant. Lower current reduces conductor heating and can reduce the amount of conductor material needed. But equipment downstream has its own permitted input voltage.

A transformer connects these requirements. Changing magnetic fields couple its windings, allowing it to change alternating-current voltage while keeping the electricity AC. It does not perform the AC-to-DC conversion that a conventional server power supply performs.

A facility can therefore move electricity at higher voltage and step it down closer to its users. The transformer still has a finite rating. A larger utility agreement cannot make an installed transformer carry unlimited load.

The breaker that stopped the job

Switchgear lets operators connect, disconnect and isolate electrical circuits. Protective relays detect abnormal conditions and can command circuit breakers to interrupt fault currents. Opening a breaker may be the correct response to a fault; allowing current to continue could damage equipment.

